# Use collection abc for generics

<rule>
name: python-collections-abc-for-generics
description: |
    - When annotating generic types such as iterables, sequences, mappings, sets, and callables, always use the abstract base classes (ABCs) from the `collections.abc` module (e.g., `Iterable`, `Sequence`, `Mapping`, `Set`, `Callable`) instead of their counterparts from the `typing` module or built-in types.
    - This practice ensures forward compatibility and adherence to the latest Python standards, as the `typing` module's generic collections (e.g., `typing.List`, `typing.Dict`) are deprecated in favor of built-in types and `collections.abc` generics since Python 3.9.
    - For example, use `from collections.abc import Iterable, Sequence, Mapping, Set, Callable` and annotate as `Iterable[int]`, `Mapping[str, float]`, etc.
    - This applies to all function signatures, variable annotations, and class attributes.
    - For more details, see the [official Python documentation on collections.abc](mdc:https:/docs.python.org/3/library/collections.abc.html).

actions:
    - type: general_guidance
      command: |
        Use `collections.abc` ABCs for all generic type annotations in Python code.

examples:
    - input: |
        # Old/deprecated style
        from typing import List, Dict, Callable
        def process(items: List[int], config: Dict[str, float], cb: Callable[[int], str]) -> None:
            pass

        # Recommended style (Python 3.9+)
        from collections.abc import Iterable, Mapping, Callable
        def process(items: Iterable[int], config: Mapping[str, float], cb: Callable[[int], str]) -> None:
            pass

metadata:
    priority: high
    version: 1.0
</rule> 